     "Hello dear,<div>I am developing a scale to measure family interaction among people with psychosis. the measure has 25 items and 401 people participated in the study</div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">I chose to fit a bi-factor model for its theoretical proximity with the concept.  we hypothesize that even though there could be specific domains of interaction, all items in the scale can also be affected by a latent general overarching construct(the general family communication factor). Here is the syntax:</span></div><div># bifcator CFA<br>model&lt;- &#39;genf =~ fci1+fci2+fci3+fci4+fci5+fci6+fci7+fci8+fci9+fci10+fci11+fci12+fci13+<br>fci14+fci15+fci16+fci17+fci18+fci19+fci20+fci22+fci23+fci24+fci25<br>factor1 =~ fci1 + fci2 + fci3+fci4+fci5+fci6+fci7+fci8+fci9+fci10+fci11+fci12+fci13<br>factor2 =~ fci14+fci15+fci16+fci17+fci18+fci19+fci20+fci22+fci23+fci24+fci25<br>&#39;<br>bifactor_model &lt;- cfa(model, data = cfaa, orthogonal = TRUE, ordered = TRUE)<br>summary(bifactor_model, standardized = TRUE, fit.measures = TRUE)<span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\"><br></span></div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\"> Kindly answer the following questions by looking at the r-output</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">1. is it ok that standard errors of the residuals and intercepts are not reported, but the factor variance errors are reported</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">2. the p.value for the variance of factor 2 is not significant, does this mean I have to drop this specific factor</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">3. what do the scales y* indicate</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">4. Model fit indices are many: there are scaled, standard, and robust. which fit indices should I use</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">5. anything that you think would help improve the scale, recommendations or changes should I make</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\"><br></span></div><div>lavaan 0.6.16 ended normally after 133 iterations<br><br>  Estimator                                       DWLS<br>  Optimization method                           NLMINB<br>  Number of model parameters                       120<br><br>  Number of observations                           401<br><br>Model Test User Model:<br>                                              Standard      Scaled<br>  Test Statistic                               358.004     580.299<br>  Degrees of freedom                               228         228<br>  P-value (Chi-square)                           0.000       0.000<br>  Scaling correction factor                                  0.712<br>  Shift parameter                                           77.586<br>    simple second-order correction                                <br><br>Model Test Baseline Model:<br><br>  Test statistic                             22593.489    7768.749<br>  Degrees of freedom                               276         276<br>  P-value                                        0.000       0.000<br>  Scaling correction factor                                  2.979<br><br>User Model versus Baseline Model:<br><br>  Comparative Fit Index (CFI)                    0.994       0.953<br>  Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI)                       0.993       0.943<br>                                                                  <br>  Robust Comparative Fit Index (CFI)                         0.866<br>  Robust Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI)                            0.837<br><br>Root Mean Square Error of Approximation:<br><br>  RMSEA                                          0.038       0.062<br>  90 Percent confidence interval - lower         0.030       0.056<br>  90 Percent confidence interval - upper         0.045       0.068<br>  P-value H_0: RMSEA &lt;= 0.050                    0.998       0.001<br>  P-value H_0: RMSEA &gt;= 0.080                    0.000       0.000<br>                                                                  <br>  Robust RMSEA                                               0.093<br>  90 Percent confidence interval - lower                     0.084<br>  90 Percent confidence interval - upper                     0.102<br>  P-value H_0: Robust RMSEA &lt;= 0.050                         0.000<br>  P-value H_0: Robust RMSEA &gt;= 0.080                         0.991<br><br>Standardized Root Mean Square Residual:<br><br>  SRMR                                           0.052       0.052<br><br>Parameter Estimates:<br><br>  Standard errors                           Robust.sem<br>  Information                                 Expected<br>  Information saturated (h1) model        Unstructured<br><br>Latent Variables:<br>                   Estimate  Std.Err  z-value  P(&gt;|z|)   Std.lv  Std.all<br>  genf =~                                                               <br>    fci1              1.000                               0.532    0.532<br>    fci2              0.627    0.108    5.788    0.000    0.334    0.334<br>    fci3              1.242    0.120   10.375    0.000    0.661    0.661<br>    fci4              0.846    0.115    7.383    0.000    0.450    0.450<br>    fci5              0.879    0.106    8.310    0.000    0.468    0.468<br>    fci6              0.937    0.109    8.589    0.000    0.499    0.499<br>    fci7              1.238    0.120   10.297    0.000    0.659    0.659<br>    fci8              1.303    0.125   10.435    0.000    0.693    0.693<br>    fci9              1