  {
   "id": "7ZjV0HDRAAAJ",
   "from": "Dessalegn Kiross",
   "author": "63e835b39774f1f4",
   "date": "2023-11-30T20:26:12Z",
   "subject": "Re: \"bifactor CFA model using DWLS estimator\" r-output interpretation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you Daniel for the helpful feedback,<div><br><div><br><div> </div></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, Nov 28, 2023 at 11:44 PM Daniel Morillo Cuadrado &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">danielmo...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">El mar, 28 nov 2023 a las 10:51, Dessalegn Kiross (&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">dessa...@gmail.com</a>&gt;) escribió:<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\"> Kindly answer the following questions by looking at the r-output</span><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">1. is it ok that standard errors of the residuals and intercepts are not reported, but the factor variance errors are reported</span></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Totally ok. The residuals are identified by the &quot;scale&quot; (see answer to 3) and the factor loadings, so they are not actually estimated, neither are the intercepts as they would not be identified.<br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">2. the p.value for the variance of factor 2 is not significant, does this mean I have to drop this specific factor</span></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>You can try and then run a likelihood ratio test between the two models. If you lose fit you have evidence that factor 2 is actually necessary to have a good model fit. Nevertheless, I&#39;d argue that it is also important to take into account theoretical aspects when making that decision.<br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">3. what do the scales y* indicate</span></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>That is the variance of the &quot;latent utility&quot;. This implies that the variance resulting from summing up the effect of all the factors + the uniqueness of the items is fixed to 1 for all of them. Similarly, the intercepts, fixed to 0, are interpreted as the mean of the distribution of that same latent utility. These two constraints (for each item) are necessary for interpretation.<br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">4. Model fit indices are many: there are scaled, standard, and robust. which fit indices should I use</span></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Although I&#39;m sure you&#39;ll receive better suggestions, you need to consider at least CFI, TLI, (robust) RMSEA, and SRMR.<br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;font-size:14.6667px\">5. anything that you think would help improve the scale, recommendations or changes should I make</span></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>I&#39;d only try to fix the variances of the factors to 1 and free the loadings of the first item for each latent factor. Although it&#39;s a matter of preference, I find it easier to understand in the case of latent variable modeling with ordinal indicators, such as your case.</div><div><br></div></div><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><font size=\"2\"><span>Hope it helps!<br></span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span>--</span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">Daniel Morillo, Ph.D.</span></font></div><div><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"></span></font></div><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">\r\n<a href=\"https://github.com/DaniMori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/DaniMori&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792415210591000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Ua6UQsjaTfmbOk-ZUDwh6\">GitHub</a> | <a href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3021-3878\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3021-3878&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792415210591000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2GeKe0fJ4lKrzajq2X01at\">\r\nORCID</a></span></font></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
